Asymmetrical pigmented lesion, Irregular borders, Variegated colour/Evolution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-028
Reason for Visit: Sudden spontaneous bleeding from a rapidly changing, darkly pigmented skin lesion causing severe patient panic.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the sudden onset of bleeding from a dark, irregularly shaped mole that the patient noted had doubled in size over a few weeks. Urgent assessment was necessary to achieve hemostasis, formally assess the lesion for high-risk features of invasive melanoma, and initiate an immediate, high-priority surgical referral pathway, averting severe ongoing anxiety and ensuring no delay in definitive management before the next available routine appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "This black mole on my back just started bleeding onto my shirt today, and it looks completely different than it did a month ago. I'm terrified it's cancer."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a deeply concerning, asymmetrical pigmented skin lesion on the upper back. The patient states it was previously a small, flat brown freckle but has undergone rapid evolution over the past 8 weeks, notably increasing in diameter and becoming elevated. Today, the patient noted a new onset of localized pruritus followed by spontaneous bleeding from the pre-existing mole, prompting severe panic. The spouse notes the 'ugly duckling' sign, stating the lesion distinctly stands out from the patient's other benign nevi. The patient has a history of severe sunburns in their youth.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme anxiety and distress regarding a highly probable malignant diagnosis.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, pacing the room, highly anxious regarding the diagnosis.
Physical Exam: On the intermittently sun-exposed left upper back, there is an 8x9 millimetre asymmetrical pigmented skin lesion. The borders are distinctly irregular, notched, and scalloped. There is a highly variegated colour distribution including dark black, brown, grey, and an erythematous hue at the periphery. The center of the lesion is elevated and demonstrates a small, 2mm focal area of active serosanguinous oozing/bleeding. There is no surrounding cellulitis. Palpation of the bilateral axillary and cervical lymph node basins reveals no palpable regional lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Patient has applied multiple tissues to the back to stop the bleeding.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current anticoagulant medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Malignant Melanoma (Nodular/Superficial Spreading) with acute ulceration - Status: Acute, high priority
Differentials:

Seborrheic Keratosis (Traumatized) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The lack of a classic "stuck-on" waxy appearance, the highly irregular borders, and the presence of multiple dark colours (grey/black) which are highly suspicious for melanocytic atypia.

Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The rapid change in size over weeks (BCCs are typically much slower growing) and the lack of classic pearly rolled borders or arborizing vessels.

Atypical Nevus (Dysplastic) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute spontaneous bleeding, the extreme variegated color including black/grey, and rapid evolution, which heavily favor invasive malignancy over a simple dysplastic mole.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (due to psychological distress and life-threatening potential of the lesion)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an actively bleeding, rapidly evolving pigmented lesion meeting all ABCDE criteria for malignant melanoma. Triage accurately prioritized the spontaneous bleeding and extreme patient panic as an urgent scenario. The immediate clinical necessity was two-fold: physically managing the active bleeding from the ulcerated tumor, and providing explicit, structured clinical direction regarding the high index of suspicion for melanoma. Delaying this assessment would have left the patient in a state of extreme, unmanaged panic with an actively bleeding lesion. Out-of-hours intervention achieved hemostasis and expedited the definitive surgical excision pathway.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Achieved complete hemostasis utilizing firm direct pressure and the application of an alginate dressing. Secured a robust, non-adherent dressing over the lesion to protect it from further trauma. Photographed the lesion (with patient consent) for the referral letter.
Positive Behavioral Change: By keeping this heavy dressing completely intact and dry until your surgical appointment, you will protect the fragile surface of the spot, preventing any further frightening bleeding episodes. By attending the urgent surgical excision appointment exactly as scheduled, you are taking the definitive, necessary step to physically remove the problem and ensure your long-term health.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Initiated urgent fast-track referral to a specialist General Surgeon or Dermatologist for full-thickness excisional biopsy.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours to confirm the specialist appointment has been booked and to provide ongoing psychological support.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the lesion begins bleeding heavily and soaking through the dressing, and it does not stop after 20 minutes of firm, uninterrupted direct pressure, present to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical features are highly suspicious for a skin cancer. Definitive diagnosis and staging can only be made after the entire lesion is surgically removed and examined under a microscope.
Clinical Course: The bleeding is a mechanical symptom of the fragile surface and is now controlled. The crucial next step is complete surgical excision within the next 1-2 weeks.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to change the dressing yourself or inspect the lesion, as this will trigger fresh bleeding. Apply continuous pressure if bleeding restarts.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The mole has changed rapidly and developed fragile blood vessels, which is why it bled today. The thick dressing acts as a protective shield. The only correct treatment is for a surgeon to cut out the entire spot so it can be tested in a laboratory.
Actionable Steps: Do not scratch, bump, or wet the dressing. Keep your phone on loud tomorrow to receive the urgent specialist appointment call.

